## Run the backend

The generated `{{packageName}}.Application` has a `main` method. The community kernel driver reads
its configuration from system properties named `exeris.<key>` and environment variables named
`EXERIS_<KEY>`, and from no file. `exeris.http.port` starts the HTTP server on that port, and
`exeris.persistence.runMigrations` creates the kernel's own tables at start. The application's own
tables come from the generated migrations in `src/main/generated/java/db/migration`. They are
PostgreSQL SQL, and the kernel does not apply them: apply them with Flyway or `psql`.
{{#postgresql}}
| System property | Value |
|---|---|
| `exeris.http.port` | `{{httpPort}}` |
| `exeris.persistence.jdbcUrl` | `{{jdbcUrl}}` |
| `exeris.persistence.username` | `exeris` |
| `exeris.persistence.password` | `exeris` |
| `exeris.persistence.runMigrations` | `true` |

From the project root, after a build:

```bash
docker compose up -d --wait
for migration in {{backendDir}}src/main/generated/java/db/migration/V*.sql; do
  docker compose exec -T postgres psql -q -U exeris -d {{databaseName}} -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 < "$migration"
done
{{#e2e}}cd backend
{{/e2e}}mvn dependency:build-classpath -DincludeScope=runtime -Dmdep.outputFile=target/classpath.txt
java -Dexeris.http.port={{httpPort}} \
    -Dexeris.persistence.jdbcUrl={{jdbcUrl}} \
    -Dexeris.persistence.username=exeris -Dexeris.persistence.password=exeris \
    -Dexeris.persistence.runMigrations=true \
    -cp "target/classes:$(cat target/classpath.txt)" {{packageName}}.Application
```
{{/postgresql}}{{^postgresql}}
H2 builds the project and runs its generated tests with no database server, but it does not run the
generated migrations, so an application started on it has no tables of its own. To run the
application, generate the project with `--database=postgresql`; its README starts the database,
applies the migrations and starts the application.
{{/postgresql}}